@inject StaticAssetVersion AssetVersion
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#12141a" />
    @* A hand-authored SVG vinyl-record mark (amber ring/label/spindle-hole on the site's own navy,
       spec 10.2's palette) - no third-party asset, no license to track. SVG is the primary icon
       (scalable, modern browser support); the 32x32 PNG is the fallback for the handful of
       contexts that still need a raster favicon; apple-touch-icon.png (180x180, Apple's required
       size) is for iOS/iPadOS "Add to Home Screen", which does not accept SVG at all. All three
       generated from the identical geometry (see git history for the one-off ImageSharp script),
       not drawn separately, so they can never visually drift from each other. *@
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon-32.png" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    @* Spec section 10.2: dark-first design, so the compiled Tailwind stylesheet (US-013 AC1) is
       the only stylesheet the app ever links - no inline <style> blocks or style="" attributes
       anywhere in Components/, since CSP's style-src is 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline' (US-012).
       2026-09-06: the "?v=" content-hash query string (StaticAssetVersion) is cache-busting - a
       plain `UseStaticFiles()` sets no explicit Cache-Control, so without a URL that changes when
       the file's bytes change, a browser can keep serving an old cached copy past a deploy (a
       mismatched "new HTML, old CSS" screenshot is exactly what surfaced this). *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@($"/css/app.css?v={AssetVersion.For("css/app.css")}")" />
    @* US-019: progressive enhancement only - the home page's "Show more playlists" link (a plain
       GET, see PlaylistCataloguePage) works with zero JavaScript; this external, same-origin
       script (CSP's script-src 'self' has no 'unsafe-inline', so it can't be inlined) automates
       clicking that link via IntersectionObserver. `defer` keeps it non-blocking and off the
       critical rendering path; it silently no-ops on every page without a ".load-more" element. *@
    <script src="@($"/js/infinite-scroll.js?v={AssetVersion.For("js/infinite-scroll.js")}")" defer></script>
    @* US-011 AC1: the per-page <PageTitle> (every route sets one) is the single source of <title> -
       a static fallback here would render as a second, duplicate <title> element alongside it
       (verified empirically), which is exactly what AC1's "unique <title> per page" rules out. *@
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>
<body>
    <SiteHeader />
    <main class="site-main">
        <Routes />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
</body>
</html>
